WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:07.020
The fetch, decode, execute cycle: how a CPU runs a program, one instruction at a time.

00:08.233 --> 00:12.201
A CPU runs a program by repeating three steps.

00:12.501 --> 00:15.232
Fetch: get the next instruction from memory.

00:15.682 --> 00:18.669
Decode: work out what the instruction means.

00:19.119 --> 00:21.700
Execute: carry the instruction out.

00:22.150 --> 00:26.587
Then it starts again. This happens billions of times every second.

00:28.050 --> 00:34.877
Here is a simplified computer: the CPU on the left, and main memory (RAM) on the right.

00:35.617 --> 00:42.251
RAM holds the program's instructions and its data. Every location has its own address.

00:43.017 --> 00:47.113
The Program Counter holds the address of the next instruction.

00:48.017 --> 00:54.374
The Memory Address Register holds the address the CPU is about to read from, or write to.

00:55.117 --> 01:01.197
The Memory Data Register holds the instruction or data that has just been fetched from memory.

01:01.950 --> 01:06.942
The Control Unit decodes each instruction and coordinates the other parts.

01:07.683 --> 01:13.934
The ALU does the arithmetic and logic. Its results are stored in the Accumulator.

01:14.683 --> 01:19.803
FETCH. The Program Counter holds 0: the address of the next instruction.

01:20.550 --> 01:23.558
That address is copied into the MAR.

01:24.550 --> 01:29.713
It travels along the address bus, and memory location 0 is selected.

01:30.450 --> 01:35.783
The instruction stored there comes back along the data bus, into the MDR.

01:36.517 --> 01:41.743
The Program Counter increases by 1, so it now points to the next instruction.

01:42.517 --> 01:47.765
DECODE. The instruction is passed to the Control Unit, which works out what it means.

01:48.883 --> 01:55.305
LDA means load a value into the Accumulator. 5 is the address of that value.

01:56.050 --> 01:59.378
EXECUTE. The address 5 is placed in the MAR...

02:00.250 --> 02:03.258
...and memory location 5 is selected.

02:04.017 --> 02:08.475
The data stored there, the number 7, is copied into the MDR...

02:09.317 --> 02:13.903
...and then loaded into the Accumulator. The instruction is complete.

02:14.650 --> 02:18.149
Now the cycle starts again with the next instruction.

02:18.883 --> 02:25.219
FETCH. The address in the Program Counter, 1, goes to the MAR and along the address bus.

02:25.983 --> 02:31.402
The instruction ADD 6 comes back along the data bus, into the MDR.

02:32.150 --> 02:34.987
The Program Counter increases to 2.

02:35.750 --> 02:41.617
DECODE. The Control Unit decodes it: add the number at address 6 to the Accumulator.

02:42.350 --> 02:45.358
EXECUTE. The address 6 is sent to memory...

02:46.850 --> 02:50.882
...and the data stored there, 5, is fetched into the MDR.

02:51.783 --> 02:56.413
The ALU adds it to the 7 that is already in the Accumulator.

02:57.417 --> 03:01.086
The result, 12, is stored back in the Accumulator.

03:01.917 --> 03:05.117
FETCH. Third cycle: address 2 goes to memory...

03:05.917 --> 03:11.271
...the instruction STA 7 comes back, and the Program Counter becomes 3.

03:12.017 --> 03:17.158
DECODE. STA means store: put the Accumulator's value into address 7.

03:17.917 --> 03:23.997
EXECUTE. The address 7 goes to the MAR, and the value 12 goes to the MDR.

03:24.750 --> 03:31.406
The data bus carries it to memory location 7. The answer to 7 + 5 has been saved.

03:32.183 --> 03:38.498
The next instructions are OUT, which outputs the 12, and HLT, which halts the program.

03:39.900 --> 03:49.820
Fetch. The address in the PC is copied to the MAR. The instruction is fetched from memory into the MDR. The PC increases by 1.

03:50.320 --> 03:55.973
Decode. The Control Unit works out what the instruction means, and what data it needs.

03:56.473 --> 04:04.367
Execute. The instruction is carried out. Calculations are done by the ALU. Results are stored in the Accumulator.

04:04.867 --> 04:13.720
Clock speed is the number of cycles per second. A 3 GHz CPU can carry out about 3 billion cycles every second.
